    content: "When Non-Resident Indians (NRIs) sell luxury real estate in India, Indian tax law mandates a statutory Tax Deducted at Source (TDS) deduction of 20% (plus applicable surcharge and cess, scaling up to 23.92%) on the **total sale consideration** under Section 195, rather than on actual net capital gains. Without proactive tax planning, massive capital can remain locked in tax refund queues for over 18 months.\n\nAdroit Real Estate Services outlines the statutory roadmap to legally minimize TDS and repatriate sale proceeds seamlessly under FEMA guidelines.\n\n1. Securing a Lower TDS Certificate (Form 13)\n\n• Section 195 Application: Apply for a Lower TDS Certificate from the Income Tax Department prior to executing the registered sale deed. This allows TDS to be computed strictly on actual capital gains (factoring in indexed cost of acquisition and Section 54 reinvestments) rather than the gross sale amount.\n• Processing Window: Typically takes 4 to 6 weeks; must be initiated during transaction negotiation.\n\n2. NRO to NRE Banking & Form 15CA/15CB Execution\n\n• Remittance up to USD 1 Million: Under RBI's Liberalized Remittance Scheme (LRS), NRIs can repatriate up to USD 1,000,000 per financial year from their NRO account to foreign bank accounts.\n• Form 15CB Certification: Certified by an Indian Chartered Accountant confirming all capital gains taxes have been paid.\n• Form 15CA Electronic Filing: Completed on the income tax portal and submitted to the authorized dealer bank to execute the foreign outward wire.\n\nNRI Advisory Desk at Adroit Real Estate Services\n\nAdroit Real Estate Services maintains a dedicated NRI wealth desk coordinating legal diligence, Chartered Accountant certifications, and Sub-Registrar executions for clients across the US, UK, UAE, and Singapore.\n\nContact Adroit Real Estate Services for seamless NRI real estate transaction management."
